+| `LIGHTNING-K12` | Verified / UE 5.8.1, vibeUEProfile=niagara-authoring (composite 4612cc04) | `NiagaraScratchPadService` cannot author scratch modules inside a **standalone NiagaraEmitter asset**. `RegisterScratchModuleForEmitter` builds the `UEA_ScratchHost` as a NiagaraSystem whose SystemSpawn/SystemUpdate graphs hold no emitter node, and `ApplyChanges` validates those two scripts as well, so it fails unconditionally with `[SystemSpawn/SystemSpawnScript] Graph contains no nodes! Please add an emitter.` (×2, plus SystemUpdate ×2). The edited scratch graph therefore never reaches the module signature and the stack's function-call node stays stale: `Function call is stale and needs to be refreshed` ×3, `Incorrect number of outputs. Can possibly be fixed with a graph refresh.`, `Error compiling Pin - Node: Output Particle Spawn Pin: Out`. Consequence chain: the module instance reports `inputs: []`, so every `SetStackInputData` fails `Input '<name>' not found in stack reference`. `GetStackIssues` returns `numErrors=0` with `fixes: []` for this, so `ApplyStackIssueFix` is not a lever, and `SetModuleEnabled` off→on plus RemoveModule/AddModule retries do not refresh the signature. The same tools work on NiagaraSystem assets. Author scratch modules in a system package, or extend the plugin to skip/host-pad the scratch host's system scripts. **Corrected 2026-09-02**: the "never reaches the module signature" half is wrong. Authoring the module with proper stack inputs (`AddModuleInput` -> `Module.X`, read by a MapGet) and particle outputs (`AddModuleOutput` with fully qualified `Particles.X`) instead of bare `AddParameterInputNode` Input nodes plus `Output.X` MapSet targets **does** push the signature through — the rebuilt `LG_CtrlSpec` reached zero errors on `SpawnScript_1_0` with the stale / incorrect-number-of-outputs / VM diagnostics all cleared, and the wrong `Output.X` target had also been inventing five extra signature outputs. What still holds: `ApplyChanges` returns False and issues no save token because the wrapper's empty system scripts are validated, so persisting a standalone emitter asset still requires the user's Ctrl+S. Also record the vocabulary limit, corrected against `UNiagaraScratchPadService.cpp:198` (`ResolveType`): it accepts `float`, `int`/`int32`, `bool`, `vec2`/`vector2`, `vec3`/`vector`/`vector3`, `vec4`/`vector4`, **`position`**, `color`/`LinearColor`, and `ArrayFloat`/`ArrayFloat2`/`ArrayVec2`/`ArrayVector`/`ArrayFloat3`/`ArrayVec3`/`ArrayFloat4`/`ArrayVec4`. The real constraint is that it wants these short vocabulary names, not Niagara display type names — `NiagaraFloat` and `Vector3f` report Unknown TypeName even though `GetNodePins` reports pin types in that longer form. An earlier note here listed only the first five; `position` in particular matters because it lets a scratch pin connect straight to a `User.*Position` MapGet output with no `Position -> Vector` convert node. |
+| `LIGHTNING-K13` | Verified / UE 5.8.1 | A scratch script path is **package-private**, so `AddModule(moduleAsset=GetScratchScriptPath(...))` across packages makes the consumer unsavable. The returned refPath has the shape `/Game/A/A:UEA_ScratchHost.<Module>`; installing it in package B sets `NiagaraNodeFunctionCall.FunctionScript` to a private object of A, and every save (manual and Autosave) fails with `Illegal reference to private object: 'NiagaraSystem /Game/A/A:UEA_ScratchHost' referenced by 'NiagaraNodeFunctionCall_N' ... in its 'FunctionScript' property`, with `LogSavePackage: Warning: SavePackage Invalid Reference Diagnostics` naming the instigator. The consuming system has no scratch copy of its own — `GetScratchScriptPath(systemPath=<consumer>)` resolves through the source emitter asset and returns the same foreign path — so there is no local-path variant to substitute. Repair: `RemoveModule` that module from the consumer, independently read back `GetScriptStackTopology` (`modules: []`) and `GetCompileMessages` (zero errors), then save. Note the token race: a save token must come from the immediately preceding command, because an interleaved read that triggers a Niagara recompile bumps the package generation and yields `SAVE_SNAPSHOT_CONFLICT`; a `GetScriptStackTopology` described as `effect=read` nonetheless returned `effect=changed` and issued a usable token. |
