+| `CORE-20260902-GETCONTENT-RAW-CONVERTTOJSON-DEPTH` | Verified / Windows PowerShell 5.1 | `Get-Content -Raw` returns a PSObject carrying provider NoteProperties (`PSPath`, `PSParentPath`, `PSChildName`, `PSDrive`, `PSProvider`, `ReadCount`), not a plain String. Passing it to `ConvertTo-Json` at `-Depth` >= 4 serializes those properties recursively and grows exponentially: a 754-char HLSL body gave 4.5 KiB at depth 4, 39 KiB at 5, 475 KiB at 6, 107 MiB at 8, and never returned at 10 or 40 — two runs burned ~640 CPU-seconds each before a file-based trace located it. Controls isolate the cause: a 754-char literal, and the same body after any `-replace`, both serialize as a JSON string at every depth, because those yield a plain String; a short sentinel string is stable at depths 2-6, so this is not generic depth behavior. Fix: `[string](Get-Content -Raw ...)`, verified safe at `-Depth 40`. Prior scripts using `-Depth 40` survived only because their payloads had passed through a string operation. Recognize the symptom as: the request JSON file is never written, the process pins one core, and stdout shows nothing because the pipeline consumer is still buffering. |
+| `NIAGARA-20260902-APPLYCHANGES-DIRTIES-ENGINE-PACKAGE` | Observed / UE 5.8.1, VibeUE niagara-authoring | `NiagaraScratchPadService.ApplyChanges` on a system also dirtied the engine plugin content package `/Niagara/Modules/Emitter/EmitterState`, so the kernel returned `outcome=result_unknown`, `persistence=dirty_out_of_scope`, error "The command dirtied a package outside its declared write scopes", and no token. The refusal is protective — that package lives under the shared engine install. `NiagaraToolset_System` writes on the same system (`AddUserVariables`, `RemoveUserVariables`, `SetEmitterData`, `SetUserParameterCategory`) dirtied only the declared package and issued clean tokens, and `SetSimTarget` recompiled without dirtying it, so the dirt tracks the scratch ApplyChanges path rather than recompilation generally. Route: let ApplyChanges apply (it still returns True and the change lands — confirm by compile messages plus HLSL readback), then take the token from a following `NiagaraToolset_System` write carrying `allow_preexisting_dirty_save=true` and save only the project package. A later ApplyChanges in the same session returned `succeeded` without re-dirtying it, so treat the refusal as recoverable. Never declare the engine package in scopes: `ueagent_save.packages` must equal the token set, so it would be written. |
+| `CORE-20260902-SAVE-TOKEN-FOLLOWS-OBSERVED-EFFECT` | Verified / UE 5.8.1 reliable queue | Token issuance follows the receipt's **observed** `effect`, not the tool's described one, and `allow_preexisting_dirty_save=true` only decides whether already-dirty declared packages may enter the token set — it cannot make a `no_change` command issue one. Measured: `GetScratchScriptPath` (allow-listed, `effect=no_change`) returned `hasToken=False` with the flag set, while `SetSimTarget`, `AddUserVariables`, `RemoveUserVariables`, and `SetUserParameterCategory` all returned `effect=changed` with `hasToken=True`. Note `SetSimTarget` and `GetEmitterTopology` are described `effect=read` yet are not on the read-only allow-list and do report `changed`. Consequence: never plan a read as the token carrier; end the batch with a small genuinely-needed write (a parameter-grouping call works well) and save immediately after it. Sharpens LIGHTNING-K09's "use a mutation that ends changed" and the note at the end of LIGHTNING-K13. |
+| `NIAGARA-20260903-ADDEMITTER-DEEPCOPIES-SCRATCH` | Verified / UE 5.8.1 | `NiagaraToolset_System.AddEmitter(templateEmitter=<standalone NiagaraEmitter asset>)` **deep-copies** the emitter and its scratch modules into the consumer's own package, so it does not hit LIGHTNING-K13's cross-package private-reference failure: `GetScratchScriptPath` on the consumer returned `/Game/LightningToolkit/NS_LG_Bolt_V7.NS_LG_Bolt_V7:NE_Controller.NiagaraScratchPadContainer_1.LG_CtrlSpec_0`, `ListScratchModules` reported the module as owned, the graph arrived intact (6 nodes including the `Util::ExecIndex` op node), and the system compiled with zero errors. The boundary is the operation, not the asset: `AddModule` with a foreign scratch path references and breaks saving; `AddEmitter` with an emitter asset copies and is safe. Two caveats: the copied module's script object gains a numeric suffix while `ListScratchModules` reports the bare name, and the template's **user parameters are promoted to system level** — importing `NE_Controller` added 10 retired v2.16 parameters the consumer had deliberately removed. Read the consumer's user-variable set after any AddEmitter and reconcile; `NiagaraToolset_System` cannot clean the template itself because it rejects standalone emitter asset paths. |
+| `NIAGARA-20260903-DANGLING-PIN-RESURRECTS-PARAMS` | Verified / UE 5.8.1 | Removing user parameters is **not stable** while a scratch `MapGet` node still carries output pins named after them, and there is no RemovePin API (see also LIGHTNING-K11 on MapGet pin roles). `RemoveUserVariables` deleted 10 retired parameters (66 -> 56, compile clean, saved); the next `ApplyChanges` on that module recreated exactly those 10, ungrouped. Niagara rebuilds the module signature from the pin set and auto-creates matching User parameters. Consequences: schedule such cleanup **after** the last ApplyChanges on the module, not before, and expect to redo it if the module is authored again; a frozen asset that has not been re-applied since its parameters were retired is one ApplyChanges away from the same resurrection, so a sidecar showing the pins without the parameters is not evidence of a stable state. The dangling reads are harmless to compilation and rendering (the v3.0 kernel never references them), making this panel and spec hygiene rather than correctness. A root fix needs a RemovePin capability or rebuilding the module without those pins. |
+| `BLUEPRINT-20260904-SET-PARENT-SAVE-RESULT-UNKNOWN` | Observed / Abyss Kinesis, UE 5.8.1 | `BlueprintTools.set_parent` (`/Script/Engine.Character` -> a new plugin `ACharacter` subclass) returned `outcome=succeeded / effect=changed / verification=verified / persistence=dirty_not_saved` and issued a token, but the immediate `ueagent_save` on exactly that one package returned `SAVE_RESULT_UNKNOWN` — "the native save did not confirm every authorized package as clean", `verification=failed`, `persistence=unknown`, `unexpected_saved_packages=[]`. The `.uasset` **did** change on disk (130244 -> 131814 bytes, new sha256) and `save_generation` advanced, yet the post-save snapshot still read `dirty=True`: the reparent persisted while the package was re-dirtied between the write and the cleanliness recheck. Likely cause is the asynchronous Blueprint recompile that reparenting triggers; not isolated. Handling: treat as `RESULT_UNKNOWN` and read back `get_parent`, package dirty state, and the disk hash instead of retrying the reparent; settle with `compile_blueprint`, then re-save from a later token-carrying mutation or ask the user to save that single asset. A following `compile_blueprint` reported `effect=no_change` and `persistence=dirty_preexisting_not_saved`, so it is not itself a token carrier here. Distinct from `CORE-20260827-AUTOSAVE-SAVE-TOKEN-RACE` (`OUT_OF_BAND_SAVE`, needs no agent action between receipt and save) and from LIGHTNING-K13's `SAVE_SNAPSHOT_CONFLICT` (generation bumped by an interleaved read). |
 
